{
    "title": "Safety Zone; Stage Nine Entertainment, Sacramento NYE Fireworks, CA",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 22, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-22/pdf/2025-23551.pdf",
    "summary": "Three federal updates just landed: a labor rule was removed with claimed cost savings for small businesses, the Coast Guard will enforce a brief safety zone for Sacramento\u2019s New Year\u2019s Eve fireworks, and ocean salmon fishing rules off the West Coast are being adjusted mid-season.\n\nThe Department of Labor is removing a federal labor rule and estimates small firms will see a net $4.28 million in savings after accounting for both reduced burdens and new transition costs. This could benefit small contractors that work on federal projects by lowering compliance obligations. On the other hand, workers who had protections under the now\u2011removed rule could see fewer safeguards, and there may be uncertainty as companies and employees adjust to the change.\n\nIn Sacramento, the Coast Guard will enforce a safety zone on the river near the Tower Bridge from 8:30 to 9:45 p.m. on December 31 to support the New Year\u2019s Eve fireworks. This is meant to protect spectators, crews, and property during the show. Local residents and event organizers benefit from a safer celebration, while boaters and some commercial vessels may face short, temporary restrictions on movement in that area.\n\nAlong the West Coast, the National Marine Fisheries Service is making five in\u2011season changes to the 2025 portion of the ocean salmon fishery between the Canadian border and Cape Falcon, Oregon. These adjustments are intended to balance fishing opportunities with conservation goals as new information comes in during the season. Healthy fish populations, future fishing seasons, and the broader ecosystem stand to benefit if the measures help keep stocks within sustainable limits. At the same time, some commercial fishers and coastal businesses could experience reduced access or altered schedules that affect income in the near term.\n\nTaken together, these actions aim to cut red tape for some businesses, keep a major public event safe, and steward a key fishery\u2014while also bringing trade\u2011offs for workers, boaters, and fishing communities that will feel the immediate impacts.",
